\section{The classes}{


\describe{
\item{\code{effect_input_error}}{An argument has the wrong type, shape, or
value --- a character vector where one string was expected, a matrix
holding \code{NA}, a negative count. The caller fixes it by passing
something else. May carry \verb{$arg} (the argument name), \verb{$received} (a
short description of what arrived), and \verb{$expected} (what was wanted);
see "The three optional fields" below.}
\item{\code{effect_contract_error}}{Two objects disagree, or an object
disagrees with its own recorded identity: a result whose receipt names
a different scientific plan, a frame compiled against a different
neural domain, a signature that no longer matches the fields it
summarizes. Each object may be individually well formed; they simply do
not belong together. The fix is to pass objects that were built from
the same declarations, not to repair either one.}
\item{\code{effect_invariant_error}}{The package computed something
impossible. Nothing the caller passed can explain it, so the message
asks for a bug report rather than a change of input.}
\item{\code{effect_capability_refusal}}{Not an error in the input: the
requested interpretation cannot be earned from the objects supplied ---
for example, analytic standard errors from a fit that did not retain
the residual channel they require. Refusals carry \verb{$capability},
\verb{$namespace}, all unmet \verb{$reasons}, and concrete \verb{$remedies}. See
\code{\link[=catch_refusal]{catch_refusal()}}.}
}

The first three inherit from \code{effect_error}, so
\code{tryCatch(expr, effect_error = ...)} catches any of them while letting a
refusal through. All four inherit from \code{error}, so ordinary \code{try()} and
\code{tryCatch(expr, error = ...)} behave exactly as before.
