\section{The three optional fields}{


\verb{$arg}, \verb{$received}, and \verb{$expected} are a convenience, not a contract.
They are filled in by the shared argument guards (the \verb{.check_*} family
behind most exported entry points) and by the hand-written checks at the
entry points that know the three values, which covers the argument errors
a caller provokes in ordinary use. Every other site --- internal checks
whose failure is about a whole record rather than one argument, and checks
whose "expected" is a paragraph rather than a phrase --- leaves them
\code{NULL} and says everything in \code{conditionMessage()}.

So branch on the \strong{class} first, which is always there, and treat the
fields as something to report when present:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{if (!is.null(condition$arg)) \{
  message("bad argument `", condition$arg, "`: ", condition$received)
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The same three fields exist on \code{effect_contract_error} and
\code{effect_invariant_error} under the same rule; a contract error that
compares two identities typically fills \verb{$received} and \verb{$expected} with
the two shortened signatures.
}

